WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.329 --> 00:00:04.371
In the summer of 2017,

00:00:04.413 --> 00:00:07.538
a woman was murdered
by her partner in Sofia.

00:00:08.663 --> 00:00:11.788
The woman, let's call her "V,"

00:00:11.829 --> 00:00:15.121
was beaten for over 50 minutes

00:00:15.121 --> 00:00:16.454
before she died.

00:00:17.454 --> 00:00:19.204
The morning after,

00:00:19.204 --> 00:00:22.913
her neighbors told the press
that they heard her screams,

00:00:22.954 --> 00:00:24.538
but they didn't intervene.

00:00:25.788 --> 00:00:29.704
You see, in Bulgaria
and many other societies,

00:00:29.746 --> 00:00:33.162
domestic violence is typically seen
as a private matter.

00:00:34.037 --> 00:00:38.996
Neighbors, however, are quick to react
to any other kind of noise.

00:00:40.412 --> 00:00:44.121
We wanted to expose and affect
the absurdity of this.

00:00:44.871 --> 00:00:47.412
So we designed an experiment.

00:00:48.204 --> 00:00:52.246
We rented the apartment
just below V's for one night.

00:00:52.996 --> 00:00:54.579
And at 10pm,

00:00:54.621 --> 00:00:57.412
Maksim, the artist in our group,

00:00:57.454 --> 00:01:00.954
sat on the drum set
we had assembled in the living room

00:01:02.454 --> 00:01:04.079
and started beating it.

00:01:05.413 --> 00:01:06.829
Ten seconds.

00:01:07.913 --> 00:01:09.413
Thirty seconds.

00:01:10.288 --> 00:01:11.913
Fifty seconds.

00:01:12.913 --> 00:01:14.246
A minute.

00:01:15.079 --> 00:01:17.246
A light came on in the hallway.

00:01:18.329 --> 00:01:20.204
One minute and 20 seconds.

00:01:21.288 --> 00:01:24.829
A man was standing at the door,
hesitant to press the bell.

00:01:26.038 --> 00:01:28.538
One minute and 52 seconds.

00:01:29.163 --> 00:01:30.996
The doorbell rang,

00:01:31.996 --> 00:01:34.663
a ring that could have saved a life.

00:01:36.413 --> 00:01:39.829
"Beat." is our project exploring
the ominous silence

00:01:39.829 --> 00:01:42.079
surrounding domestic violence.

00:01:42.121 --> 00:01:45.704
We filmed the experiment,
and it became instantly viral.

00:01:45.746 --> 00:01:48.038
Our campaign amplified
the voices of survivors

00:01:48.038 --> 00:01:50.079
who shared similar stories online.

00:01:50.663 --> 00:01:52.788
It equipped neighbors
with specific advice,

00:01:52.829 --> 00:01:54.829
and many committed to taking action.

00:01:55.621 --> 00:01:58.371
In a country where every other week,

00:01:58.371 --> 00:02:01.371
the ground quietly embraces
the body of a woman

00:02:01.413 --> 00:02:04.163
murdered by a partner or a relative,

00:02:04.204 --> 00:02:05.996
we were loud,

00:02:06.038 --> 00:02:07.496
and we were heard.

00:02:10.621 --> 00:02:12.704
I am an activist,

00:02:12.746 --> 00:02:15.079
passionate about human rights innovation.

00:02:15.954 --> 00:02:19.871
I lead a global organization
for socially engaged creative solutions.

00:02:19.913 --> 00:02:24.746
In my work, I think about
how to make people care and act.

00:02:25.788 --> 00:02:30.954
I am here to tell you
that creative actions can save the world,

00:02:30.996 --> 00:02:33.621
creative actions and play.

00:02:34.579 --> 00:02:39.704
I know it is weird to talk about play
and human rights in the same sentence,

00:02:39.746 --> 00:02:41.788
but here is why it's important.

00:02:43.038 --> 00:02:46.829
More and more, we fear
that we can't win this.

00:02:47.496 --> 00:02:49.246
Campaigns feel dull,

00:02:49.288 --> 00:02:50.746
messages drown,

00:02:51.246 --> 00:02:52.788
people break.

00:02:54.079 --> 00:02:58.038
Numerous studies, including a recent one
published by Columbia University,

00:02:58.079 --> 00:03:01.746
show that burnout and depression
are widespread amongst activists.

00:03:02.413 --> 00:03:05.163
Years ago, I myself was burned out.

00:03:05.163 --> 00:03:09.621
In a world of endless ways forward,
I felt at my final stop.

00:03:10.621 --> 00:03:15.079
So what melts fear or dullness or gloom?

00:03:15.788 --> 00:03:16.996
Play.

00:03:17.454 --> 00:03:21.746
From this very stage, psychiatrist
and play researcher Dr. Stuart Brown

00:03:21.746 --> 00:03:24.538
said that nothing
lights up the brain like play,

00:03:25.579 --> 00:03:27.746
and that the opposite of play is not work,

00:03:27.746 --> 00:03:29.038
it's depression.

00:03:29.704 --> 00:03:32.079
So to pull out of my own burnout,

00:03:32.121 --> 00:03:37.746
I decided to turn my activism
into what I call today "play-tivism."

00:03:38.163 --> 00:03:39.913
(Laughter)

00:03:40.329 --> 00:03:43.579
When we play, others want to join.

00:03:44.246 --> 00:03:46.704
Today, my playground
is filled with artists,

00:03:46.746 --> 00:03:48.454
techies and scientists.

00:03:48.829 --> 00:03:51.996
We fuse disciplines
in radical collaboration.

00:03:52.663 --> 00:03:55.704
Together, we seek new ways
to empower activism.

00:03:56.329 --> 00:03:59.038
Our outcomes are not meant to be playful,

00:03:59.038 --> 00:04:01.121
but our process is.

00:04:01.829 --> 00:04:05.204
To us, play is an act of resistance.

00:04:06.204 --> 00:04:10.663
For example, "Beat.,"
the project I talked about earlier,

00:04:10.663 --> 00:04:14.913
is a concept developed by a drummer
and a software engineer

00:04:14.913 --> 00:04:18.079
who didn't know each other
two days before they pitched the idea.

00:04:19.163 --> 00:04:21.829
"Beat." is the first winner
in our lab series

00:04:21.829 --> 00:04:26.038
where we pair artists and technologists
to work on human rights issues.

00:04:26.788 --> 00:04:30.163
Other winning concepts
include a pop-up bakery

00:04:30.204 --> 00:04:34.496
that teaches about fake news through
beautiful but horrible-tasting cupcakes --

00:04:34.538 --> 00:04:37.371
(Laughter)

00:04:37.413 --> 00:04:41.288
or a board game that puts you
in the shoes of a dictator

00:04:41.288 --> 00:04:45.704
so you get to really grasp the range
of tools and tactics of oppression.

00:04:47.371 --> 00:04:50.621
We did our first lab
just to test the idea,

00:04:50.663 --> 00:04:53.413
to see where it cracks
and if we can make it better.

00:04:53.454 --> 00:04:56.704
Today, we are so in love with the format
that we put it all online

00:04:56.704 --> 00:04:58.329
for anyone to implement.

00:04:59.163 --> 00:05:02.746
I cannot overstate the value
of experimentation in activism.

00:05:03.288 --> 00:05:06.663
We can only win
if we are not afraid to lose.

00:05:08.246 --> 00:05:10.871
When we play, we learn.

00:05:12.329 --> 00:05:14.954
A recent study published
by Stanford University

00:05:14.996 --> 00:05:17.079
about the science
of what makes people care

00:05:17.079 --> 00:05:20.246
reconfirms what we have
been hearing for years:

00:05:20.288 --> 00:05:22.954
opinions are changed
not from more information

00:05:22.996 --> 00:05:25.121
but through empathy-inducing experiences.

00:05:25.996 --> 00:05:28.829
So learning from science and art,

00:05:28.829 --> 00:05:34.121
we saw that we can talk about
global armed conflict through light bulbs,

00:05:34.121 --> 00:05:36.996
or address racial inequality in the US

00:05:37.038 --> 00:05:38.496
through postcards,

00:05:39.329 --> 00:05:43.913
or tackle the lack of even
one single monument of a woman in Sofia

00:05:43.954 --> 00:05:46.038
by flooding the city with them,

00:05:46.663 --> 00:05:48.829
and, with all these works,

00:05:48.871 --> 00:05:52.288
to trigger dialogue,
understanding and direct action.

00:05:54.538 --> 00:05:58.788
Sometimes, when I talk about
taking risks and trying and failing

00:05:58.829 --> 00:06:00.579
in the context of human rights,

00:06:00.621 --> 00:06:02.954
I meet raised eyebrows,

00:06:03.621 --> 00:06:06.871
eyebrows that say, "How irresponsible,"

00:06:06.913 --> 00:06:08.913
or, "How insensitive."

00:06:09.579 --> 00:06:13.288
People often mistake play for negligence.

00:06:13.329 --> 00:06:14.621
It is not.

00:06:16.204 --> 00:06:21.038
Play doesn't just grow our armies stronger
or spark better ideas.

00:06:21.746 --> 00:06:24.163
In times of painful injustice,

00:06:24.204 --> 00:06:27.246
play brings the levity we need
to be able to breathe.

00:06:27.829 --> 00:06:30.288
When we play, we live.

00:06:33.913 --> 00:06:36.204
I grew up in a time

00:06:36.246 --> 00:06:38.121
when all play was forbidden.

00:06:39.371 --> 00:06:42.663
My family's lives were crushed
by a communist dictatorship.

00:06:43.913 --> 00:06:47.704
For my aunt, my grandfather, my father,

00:06:47.746 --> 00:06:50.204
we always held two funerals:

00:06:50.246 --> 00:06:51.621
one for their bodies,

00:06:51.621 --> 00:06:53.163
but, years before that,

00:06:53.204 --> 00:06:54.788
one for their dreams.

00:06:55.663 --> 00:06:59.371
Some of my biggest dreams are nightmares.

00:07:00.663 --> 00:07:04.954
I have a nightmare that one day
all the past will be forgotten

00:07:04.996 --> 00:07:08.204
and new clothes will be dripping
the blood of past mistakes.

00:07:09.538 --> 00:07:11.538
I have a nightmare

00:07:11.538 --> 00:07:15.038
that one day the lighthouses
of our humanity will crumble,

00:07:15.038 --> 00:07:17.329
corroded by acid waves of hate.

00:07:18.288 --> 00:07:20.913
But way more than that,

00:07:20.913 --> 00:07:22.246
I have hope.

00:07:23.163 --> 00:07:25.496
In our fights for justice and freedom,

00:07:25.496 --> 00:07:27.163
I hope that we play,

00:07:28.204 --> 00:07:32.746
and that we see the joy
and beauty of us playing together.

00:07:33.621 --> 00:07:35.038
That's how we win.

00:07:36.038 --> 00:07:37.288
Thank you.

00:07:37.871 --> 00:07:40.746
(Applause)

